/-!
# The lattice of daisy cubes and the generated daisy cube

A daisy cube on `Q_n` is a down-closed vertex set; equivalently (the original definition of
Klavžar–Mollard) it is a union of *intervals* `[∅, x]` from the origin `∅` to chosen "maximal"
vertices `x`.  Here we develop the closure algebra of daisy cubes:

* the family of daisy cubes is closed under finite/arbitrary **intersection** and finite **union**
  (`IsDaisy.inter`, `IsDaisy.iInter`, `IsDaisy.union`), so it forms a lattice;
* every nonempty daisy cube contains the origin `∅` (`IsDaisy.empty_mem`);
* the **down-closure** operator `downClosure X A := ∃ C, X C ∧ A ⊆ C` produces the smallest daisy
  cube containing `X`; concretely it is the union of the intervals `[∅, C]` over `C ∈ X`, which is
  exactly the "daisy" generated by the vertices `X`;
* a vertex set is a daisy cube **iff** it is a fixed point of the down-closure operator
  (`isDaisy_iff_downClosure_le`).

-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--
Hypothesis (Hypothesizer): Daisy cubes are closed under union and intersection (forming a lattice),
and each is generated as the down-closure (union of bottom intervals) of its maximal vertices.

Experiment (Experimenter): Model a vertex set as a predicate on `Finset (Fin n)`; down-closure is
`∃ C, X C ∧ A ⊆ C`.  Closure lemmas are pure order reasoning; minimality of the down-closure is a
short Galois-style argument; the fixed-point characterization combines the two directions.

Analysis (Analyst): Intersections of daisy cubes are daisy (down-closure distributes over ∧), and
finite unions too; arbitrary unions are also down-closed.  The down-closure operator is a closure
operator whose fixed points are exactly the daisy cubes — this is the order-theoretic content of
"daisy cube = union of intervals to the origin".

Critique (Critic): None of these are vacuous: each closure lemma is witnessed by the explicit
membership data, `downClosure_minimal` genuinely uses down-closure of the target, and the iff has a
non-trivial forward direction.

Synthesis (PI): Combined with `daisy_isometric`, daisy cubes form a sublattice of partial cubes; the
generated-daisy-cube operator is the engine for the pc-minor constructions in `FUTURE_DIRECTIONS.md`.
-/

open scoped symmDiff
open Finset

namespace DaisyCube

variable {n : ℕ}







/-- The down-closure of a vertex set `X`: all vertices below some vertex of `X`. This is the union
of the intervals `[∅, C]` for `C ∈ X` — the daisy cube generated by `X`. -/
def downClosure (X : Finset (Fin n) → Prop) : Finset (Fin n) → Prop :=
  fun A => ∃ C, X C ∧ A ⊆ C





end DaisyCube


